        "title": "Tropical Tree Diversity: Rainforest Structure, Emergents, Epiphytes",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Tropical rainforests cover ~7% of Earth's land surface but contain ~50% of terrestrial species. Their architecture is vertical — multiple strata from ground to emergent crowns — and their diversity is extreme: a single hectare of Amazonian forest may contain 300+ tree species, compared to ~30 in a temperate forest hectare. The drivers of this diversity and the structural features that sustain it are the focus of this guide."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-TROPICAL-TREES.md#003",
      "path": [
